    "schema": "minireason.language-corpus.v1",
    "text": "A replicated warehouse snapshot is not a single ledger but a cloud of rival stories about what has been promised. The capacity and logical time are shared coordinates, but the records arrive as a bag: orderless, possibly repeated, possibly stale, possibly contradictory. The first useful conjecture is that the snapshot's job is not to compute one timeless answer but to decide which conflicts are still alive. Duplicate delivery of the same booking, revision, kind, quantity, and expiry adds nothing; authority does not accumulate by repetition. A higher revision kills lower revisions for that booking, but only within the booking. Two bookings can disagree about which revision they have accepted without infecting each other.\n\nFor any one booking, the natural reconstruction is to group its records by revision, then keep only the greatest revision present. Within that greatest revision, a hold and a release cannot both be in force as the same fact; they are alternatives. An expired hold and a release both reserve zero. An active hold reserves its quantity. If several active holds tie at the greatest revision, they remain unresolved alternatives, not a sum and not a vote. That is a deliberate refusal: the rules do not say how to merge them, so the snapshot should retain the possibility that any one is the true one, or that none is, depending on what \"true\" is taken to mean.\n\nAvailability is then capacity minus whatever reservation is actually selected, but the word \"actually\" hides the problem. If each booking's greatest revision is unambiguous, availability may still be negative if the selected holds exceed capacity. Negative availability is not an error in the arithmetic; it is a signal that the warehouse has over-promised relative to this reconstruction. If different bookings have unresolved alternatives, availability is not a number but a set or an interval, and possibly not even a convex one. A pessimistic reading takes the maximum hold among alternatives for each booking; an optimistic reading takes the minimum. Neither is forced by the snapshot alone.\n\nNow the disagreements begin.\n\nOne disagreement is whether uncertainty should be retained at all. A reconciler might collapse each tie to the largest quantity, or to the earliest expiry, or to the record with some lexical tiebreak, and thereby produce a single availability number. That is convenient, but it manufactures authority. Another reconciler might keep all alternatives and report a range. That preserves honesty but makes downstream decisions harder. The snapshot description does not settle which is correct; it only says tied records can remain unresolved. A conjecture: the more useful representation is a partial order of possible reservations, not a set, because some alternatives may be compatible with others across bookings while others are not. A criticism: once you move to a set of possible worlds, you have smuggled in a notion of possibility that the original bag did not contain.\n\nA second disagreement concerns expiry and obsolete records. If logical current time is past a hold's expiry, that hold reserves zero. But does expiry erase the record, demote it, or merely change its effect? Suppose a booking has revision 5 expired and revision 3 active. Higher revision supersedes lower revision, so revision 5 wins, and because it is expired it reserves zero. Revision 3 is obsolete and should not be resurrected. That reading treats revision as authority and expiry as effect. Another reading treats expiry as deleting the record from the bag, after which revision 3 becomes greatest and active, so the booking reserves again. These give opposite answers. The snapshot description tilts toward the first: a higher revision supersedes lower revisions, and expiry can make a hold reserve zero. It does not say expiry removes the supersession. So the first is better supported, but the second remains a live conjecture, especially if one thinks expired records are no longer \"delivered records\" in the relevant sense. A criticism of the first: it lets a dead revision keep a live one buried forever, which may be undesirable operationally.\n\nA third disagreement is what a criticism targets. One can criticize a reconstruction rule, a particular record, a booking's history, the snapshot as a whole, or the assumption that logical time is meaningful. For example, saying \"this snapshot is inconsistent\" might mean two active holds for one booking at the same greatest revision, or it might mean total reservations exceed capacity, or it might mean the logical time is behind an expiry that someone expected to have passed. These are different claims. A conjecture: many apparent disagreements about availability are really disagreements about which target a criticism addresses. A criticism of the rule \"higher revision always supersedes\" is not the same as a criticism of a record that carries a high revision but an implausible quantity. Preserving that distinction matters because one can reject a rule while accepting the data, or accept the rule while rejecting the data as corrupt or late.\n\nA fourth disagreement concerns source assumptions. The snapshot assumes records are delivered, but not that they are complete. It assumes duplicates convey no additional authority, but not that all duplicates are identical in every field; if two records share booking and revision but differ in kind or quantity, are they duplicates or alternatives? The description says duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority, which suggests exact duplicates are inert, but near-duplicates may be unresolved alternatives. It assumes logical current time is a single coordinate, but different replicas may have different logical times; if so, expiry is replica-relative. It assumes bookings are independent, but capacity is shared, so reservations interact. It assumes a release or expired hold reserves zero, but does not say whether a release at a lower revision can cancel a hold at a higher revision. By the supersession rule, no: the higher hold wins. By a cancellation rule, maybe yes. That is a source assumption about what \"release\" means.\n\nA fifth disagreement is whether availability should be computed per booking or globally. Per booking, one can say each booking's reservation is the quantity of its greatest-revision active hold, or zero otherwise, or unresolved among tied greatest-revision active holds. Globally, availability is capacity minus the sum. But if there are unresolved alternatives, the sum is not well-defined. One can compute a lower bound by taking the minimum reservation per booking and an upper bound by taking the maximum. That gives an interval, but intervals lose correlations: booking A's high alternative might be incompatible with booking B's high alternative if they share a physical resource not named in the snapshot. The snapshot does not name physical resources, so this incompatibility is invisible. A conjecture: the snapshot is under-specified for any real warehouse, and the best one can do is expose the unresolved alternatives rather than pretend to a single answer. A criticism: exposing alternatives without a policy for resolving them pushes the problem downstream, where it may be resolved badly or inconsistently.\n\nA sixth disagreement concerns negative availability. If capacity is 10 and active holds sum to 12, availability is -2. Some might call this an inconsistency and reject the snapshot. Others might call it a valid state of over-reservation. The description says availability can be negative, which suggests over-reservation is allowed. But negative availability does not say which holds are the offenders. If there are alternatives, some combinations may be negative and others not. A conjecture: the sign of availability is less important than the set of reservations that produce it, because a negative number alone does not tell you which booking to revisit. A criticism: if availability is allowed to be negative, then capacity is not a hard constraint, and the word \"capacity\" may be misleading; it is more like a soft target or a reporting threshold.\n\nA seventh disagreement is about revision gaps and missing records. Suppose a booking has revision 7 and revision 5, but no revision 6. Does revision 7 supersede revision 5? Yes, if higher revision supersedes lower. Does the absence of revision 6 matter? Not by the stated rules, but it might indicate a lost delivery. A conjecture: revision numbers are not necessarily contiguous, and treating a gap as evidence of loss is an extra assumption. A criticism: if revisions are generated in order, a gap may be meaningful, and ignoring it may hide a delivery failure.\n\nAn eighth disagreement is about tie-breaking across bookings. Different bookings need not share the same choice, so one cannot impose a global tiebreak. But a downstream consumer might want a single availability number. To get one, it must choose per booking, possibly arbitrarily. That choice is not authorized by the snapshot. A conjecture: any single-number availability is a projection that discards information, and different projections can disagree even when they respect the snapshot. A criticism: without a projection, the snapshot cannot answer the simple question \"how much is available?\" That may be acceptable if the snapshot's role is to support inquiry rather than to issue verdicts.\n\nA ninth disagreement is about time. Logical current time is given, but records have expiries. If logical time advances, expired holds become zero. Does an expired hold at a high revision still supersede a lower active hold? As above, one reading says yes. Another says expiry is a state change that makes the high revision inactive, allowing a lower revision to become relevant if it is still active. The description does not resolve this. A conjecture: the safer reading is that supersession is structural and expiry is evaluative; the high revision remains the chosen record, but its contribution is zero. A criticism: this can leave a booking with no reservation even though an older active hold exists, which may violate an operator's expectation that the warehouse still holds the goods.\n\nA tenth disagreement is about what \"delivered\" means. A record delivered to one replica may not be delivered to another. The snapshot is described as replicated, but the bag is unordered. Does the snapshot represent the union of all deliveries, or the intersection, or one replica's view? If it is a union, it may contain alternatives that no single replica has. If it is an intersection, it may omit records that matter. A conjecture: the snapshot is a merge, and merging is itself a source of uncertainty, because different replicas may have different logical times and different sets of records. A criticism: the description treats the snapshot as a single object, which may obscure the replication problem rather than solve it.\n\nFinally, there is the question of what remains material for inquiry. False or incomplete proposals are not discarded; they are part of the evidence about how people understand the system. A conjecture that a release always cancels a hold, even across revisions, may be false under the stated supersession rule, but it reveals an assumption about what releases are for. A conjecture that ties should be summed may be false under the \"unresolved alternatives\" reading, but it reveals a desire for conservatism. A criticism that the snapshot is unusable because it does not resolve ties may be substantive, but it may also be a demand for a decision that the snapshot was never meant to make. Keeping these disagreements visible is not a failure of the reconstruction; it is the reconstruction's only honest form."
